\id TIT Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Titus
\toc1 The Epistle of Paul to Titus.
\toc2 Titus
\toc3 TIT
\mt1 The Epistle of Paul to Titus.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE EPISTLE OF S. PAUL TO TITUS.
\ip THAT Titus was a Gentile, and not a Jew, and that he was in S. Paul's train, at the least the 14 year after his conversion, if not before, we understand by the Epistle to the Galatians c. 2. And that he continued with him to the very end, appeareth in the second to Timothee c. 4. Where he maketh mention that he sent him from Rome into Dalmatia, when himself was shortly after to be put to death.
\ip And therefore although S. Luke never name him in the Acts, as neither himself, yet no doubt he comprehendeth him commonly, when he speaketh thus in the first person plural: \it Forthwith we sought to go into Macedonia. Act. 16.\it* For S. Paul also sent him to Corinth, between the writing of his 1. & 2. to the Corinthians (which time concurreth with Act. 19) by occasion whereof he maketh much and honourable mention of him in the said second Epistle c. 2. & c. 7. and again he sent him \f - \ft \+it 2. Corinth. 8.\+it*\f* with the same Epistle: both times about great matters: so that no doubt he was even then also a Bishop, and received accordingly of the Corinthians, \it with fear and trembling.\it* 2. Cor. 7, v. 15. But the same is plainer in this Epistle to himself c. 1. v. 5. Where the Apostle saith: \it for this cause I left thee at Crete, &c.\it* By which words it is manifest also, that this Epistle was not written during the story of the Acts (seeing that no mention is there of S. Paul's being in the isle of Crete) but after his dismission at Rome out of his first trouble, and before his second or last trouble there, as is evident by these words: \it When I shall send to thee Artemas or Tychicus, make haste to come to me to Nicopolis, for there I have determined to winter.\it* Tit. 3.
\ip Therefore he instructeth him (and in him, all Bishops) much like as he doth Timothee, what qualities he must require in them that he shall make priests and Bishops, in what sort to preach, and to teach all sorts of men, to commend good works unto them: finally, himself to be their example in all goodness.
\c 1
\cd Of what quality the Priests and Bishops must be: 9. namely learned, considering the Judaical seducers of that time. 12. That the Cretensians must be roughly used, to have them continue sound in faith.
\p
\v 1 \sc Paul\sc* the servant of God, and an Apostle of \sc Jesus\sc* Christ according to the faith of the elect of God and knowledge of the truth: which is according to piety,
\v 2 into the hope of life everlasting, which he promised that lieth not, God, \x - \xt 2. Tim. 1, 9.\x* before the secular times:
\v 3 but hath manifested in due times his word in preaching, which is committed to me according to the precept of our Saviour God:
\v 4 to Titus my beloved son according to the common faith, grace and peace from God the Father, and Christ \sc Jesus\sc* our Saviour.
\v 5 For this cause left I thee in Crete, that thou shouldest reform the things that are wanting, and shouldest ordain priests by cities, as I also appointed thee: \ef - \fr 1.5 \fq Ordain Priests. \ft ¹ Though Priests or Bishops may be nominated and elected by the Princes, people, or Patrons of places, according to the use of the time and diversity of Countries and fashions, yet they cannot be ordered and consecrated but by a Bishop who was himself rightly ordered or consecrated before, as this Titus was by S. Paul. And here it seemeth that he did not only consecrate them whom the people had elected before, but himself also made choice of the persons, no mention being here made of any other election popular. ² Which though it were long used in the primitive Church, yet for diverse causes and specially for continual tumults, partialities, and disorders. which S. Augustine much complaineth of in his time, was justly taken away, and other better means of their designment appointed. See \+it Conc. Laodic. cap. 12. 13.\+it* S. August. \+it de adult. coniug. li. 2. c. 20. Ep. 110. and Possid. in vita Aug. c. 8.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Priests must be consecrated by Bishops only. \fq ² \ft The popular election of the Clergy taken away.\ef* \ef - \fr 1.5 \ft ¹ And that the ordering of Priests or imposition of hands to that purpose, belongeth only to Bishops, and to no inferior Priests or other persons, it is plain by the Apostolic practice set down in the Scriptures, namely in the Acts, and in the Epistles to Timothee and Titus. And S. Hierom, who seemeth sometimes to say that in the primitive Church there was no great difference betwixt a Bishop and Priest, yet he ever excepteth giving Holy Orders, which preeminence he attributeth to Bishops only. \+it ep. 85.\+it* as he doth also Confirming the Baptized by giving them the Holy Ghost through imposition of hand and holy Chrism. \+it Dial. cont. Lucifer. c. 4.\+it* ² Note also that Ærius was of old condemned of heresy, for holding that there was no difference betwixt a Priest and a Bishop. \+it Epiph. hær. 75. August. hær. 53.\+it* ³ Note lastly the fraudulent translation of the Heretics, always turning for \+it Priests\+it* (which here is evident to be a calling of Order and office) \+it elders, saying, That thou ordain Elders\+it*. which in our vulgar tongue signifieth the age, and not the Office properly: and all this for hatred of Priests. \fq ¹ \ft The preeminence of a Bishop above a Priest. \fq ² \ft To put no difference between them is Ærius' heresy. \fq ³ \ft Heret. translation.\ef*
\v 6 \x - \xt 1. Tim. 3, 2.\x* if any be without crime, the husband of one wife, having faithful children, not in the accusation of riot, or not obedient. \ef - \fr 1.6 \fq Of one wife. \ft ¹ To that which is said upon the like words \+it 1 Tim. 3:\+it* add this testimony of S. Epiphanius \+it li. 3. to. 2. cont. hæreses in fine. Holy Priesthood,\+it* saith he, \+it for the most part proceedeth of Virgins: and if not of virgins, yet of them that live a sole or single life: but and if the single and sole persons suffice not to the Ministry, of such as contain from their wives, or after once marrying remain widows. For, him that hath been married twice, it is not lawful to take to Priesthood, &c.\+it* If you list to see the causes why bigamy is forbidden them that are to be Priests, and continency required of the Clergy, see the same author \+it li. 2. to. 1. hæres. 59.\+it* S. Ambrose \+it li. 1. Offic. c. 50.\+it* and upon \+it 1 Tim. 3.\+it* S. Augustine \+it de bono Coniugal. c. 18.\+it* S. Hierom \+it ep. 50 c. 5 ad Pammachium,\+it* and against Iovinian \+it li. 1 c. 19.\+it* S. Leo \+it ep. 87.\+it* and other ancient authors. ² And if the studious reader peruse all antiquity, he shall find all notable Bishops and Priests of God's Church to have been single, or continent from their wives, if any were married before they came to the Clergy. So was S. Paul, and exhorteth all men to the like. \+it 1 Cor. 7, 7.\+it* So were all the Apostles after they followed Christ, as S. Hierom witnesseth, affirming that our Lord loved John specially for his virginity. \+it Apol. ad Pammach. c. 8. & li. 1 cont. Iovin. c. 14.\+it* S. Ignatius \+it ep. 6 ad Philadelph.\+it* saith of the said John, and of Timothee, Titus, Evodius, Clement, that they lived and died in chastity, reckoning up of the old Testament diverse notable personages that did the same, as Elias, Jesus Nave (otherwise called Josue) Melchisedec, Elisæus, Hieremie, John Baptist. No man is ignorant that all the notable fathers of the Greek and Latin Church lived chaste: Athanasius, Basil, Nazianzen, Chrysostom, Cyprian, Hilarie (who entered into Holy Orders after his wife's death) Ambrose, Hierom, Augustine, Leo, Gregory the great. Certain other notable Fathers had once wives, but no holy men ever used them, much less married, after they were in Holy Orders. ³ A marvelous thing, that so many heretofore should have the gift of chastity then, and now so few, if the Protestants say true, that scarce one among them in our age of all their sects, even of their principal Superintendents, hath had it. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Bigami\+it* excluded from holy Orders, and the causes thereof. \fq ² \ft The notable men of both Testaments, that lived continently from wives. \fq ³ \ft Only the Protestants complain that they have not the gift of chastity.\ef*
\v 7 For a Bishop must be without crime, as the steward of God: not proud, not angry, not given to wine, no striker, not covetous of filthy lucre:
\v 8 but given to hospitality, gentle, sober, just, holy, continent:
\v 9 embracing that faithful word which is according to doctrine, that he may be able to exhort in sound doctrine, and to reprove them that gainsay it.
\v 10 For there be many disobedient, vain-speakers, and seducers, especially they that are of the Circumcision.
\v 11 Who must be controlled. Who subvert whole houses, teaching the things they ought not, for filthy lucre.
\v 12 One of them said, their own proper prophet, \f 1 \fr 1.12 \ft Epimenides.\f* \it The Cretensians always liars, naughty beasts, slothful bellies.\it*
\v 13 This testimony is true. For the which cause rebuke them sharply, that they may be sound in the faith,
\v 14 not attending to Jewish fables, and commandments of men, averting themselves from the truth.
\v 15 \x - \xt Rom. 14, 20.\x* All things are \f 2 \fr 1.15 \ft He speaketh not of the Church's abstaining from meats sometimes, which is not for any uncleanness in the creatures, but for chastening their bodies: but he meaneth the Jewish superstition, who now being Christians, would not cease to put difference of clean and unclean according to their old law. See S. Augustine Cont. Faust. li. 31. c. 4.\f* clean to the clean: but to the polluted and to infidels nothing is clean: but polluted are both their mind and conscience.
\v 16 They confess that they know God: but in their works they deny, whereas they be abominable and incredulous and to every good work reprobate.
\c 2
\cd What to preach both to old and young (not only with word but with example also) and to servants. 11. For there are of all sorts in the Church, and they must be instructed accordingly.
\p
\v 1 \sc But\sc* do thou speak the things that become sound doctrine.
\v 2 Old men that they be sober, \f 1 \fr 2.2 \ft \+it c σεμνοὺς pudicos\+it*\f* chaste, wise, sound in the faith, in love, in patience.
\v 3 Old women in like manner, in holy attire, not ill speakers, not given to much wine: teaching well,
\v 4 that they may teach the young women wisdom, to love their husbands, to love their children,
\v 5 wise, chaste, sober, having a care of the house, gentle, subject to their husbands, that the word of God be not blasphemed.
\v 6 Young men in like manner exhort that they be sober.
\v 7 In all things shew thyself an example of good works, in doctrine, in integrity, in gravity,
\v 8 the word sound, irreprehensible: that he which is on the contrary part, may be afraid, having no evil to say of us.
\v 9 \x - \xt Eph. 6, 5. Col. 3, 22. 1. Pet. 2, 18.\x* Servants to be subject to their masters, in all things pleasing, not gainsaying:
\v 10 not defrauding, but in all things shewing good faith, that they may adorn the doctrine of our Saviour God in all things.
\v 11 \f 1 \fr 2.11 \ft The Epistle at the first Mass on Christmas day, and\f* For the grace of God our Saviour hath appeared to all men:
\v 12 instructing us that denying impiety and worldly desires, we live soberly, and justly, and godly in this world,
\v 13 expecting the blessed hope and \f 1 \fr 2.13 \ft \+it c ἐπιφὰνειαν\+it*\f* advent of the glory of the great God and our Saviour \sc Jesus\sc* Christ,
\v 14 who gave himself for us, that he might redeem us from all iniquity, and might cleanse to himself a people acceptable, a pursuer of good works.
\v 15 These things speak, and exhort and rebuke \f 1 \fr 2.15 \ft Bishops must be stout and command in God's cause, and the people must in nowise disobey or contemn them.\f* with all authority. Let no man contemn thee.
\c 3
\cd To teach them obedience unto Princes, and meekness towards all men, considering that we also were as they, till God of his goodness brought us to Baptism. 8. To teach good works, 9. and to avoid vain questions, 10. and obstinate Heretics.
\p
\v 1 \sc Admonish\sc* them to be subject to Princes and Potestates, to obey at a word, to be ready to every good work,
\v 2 to blaspheme no man, not to be litigious, but modest: shewing all mildness toward all men.
\v 3 For we also were sometime unwise, incredulous, erring, serving diverse desires and voluptuousnesses, living in malice and envy, odible, hating one another.
\v 4 But when \f 1 \fr 3.4 \ft b The Epistle at the 2. Mass on Christmas day, & within the octave, and in the Votive Mass of our B. Lady between Christmas and Candlemas.\f* the benignity and \f 2 \fr 3.4 \ft \+it c φιλανθρωπία\+it*\f* kindness toward man of our Saviour God appeared:
\v 5 \x - \xt 2. Tim. 1, 9.\x* not by the works of Justice which we did, but according to his mercy he hath saved us \f 2 \fr 3.5 \ft As before in the Sacrament of holy Orders \+it (1. Tim. 4. and 2. Tim. 1.)\+it* so here it is plain that Baptism giveth grace, & that by it as by an instrumental cause we be saved.\f* by the laver of regeneration and renovation of the Holy Ghost,
\v 6 whom he hath poured upon us aboundantly by \sc Jesus\sc* Christ our Saviour:
\v 7 that being justified by his grace, we may be heirs according to hope of life everlasting.
\v 8 \x - \xt 1. Tim. 4.\x* It is a faithful saying, and of these things I will have thee avouch earnestly: that they which believe in God, be careful to excel in good works. These things be good and profitable for men.
\v 9 But \x - \xt 2. Tim. 2, 23.\x* foolish questions, and genealogies, and contentions, and controversies of the Law avoid. For they are unprofitable and vain.
\v 10 Aman that is an heretic after the first and second \f 1 \fr 3.10 \ft These admonitions or correptions must be given to such as err, by our Spiritual Governors and Pastors: to whom if they yield not, Christian men must avoid them.\f* admonition avoid: \ef - \fr 3.10 \fq A man that is an Heretic. \ft ¹ Not every one that erreth in religion, is an Heretic. but he only that after the Church's determination, willfully and stubbornly standeth in his false opinion, not yielding to decree of Councel or the chief Pastors of the Church therein. \+it They\+it* (saith S. Augustine \+it ep. 162.\+it* ) \+it that defend their sentence (though false and perverse) with no stubborn stomach or obstinate heart, specially if it be such as themselves by bold presumption broached not, but received it of their deceived parents, and do seek the truth warily and carefully, being ready to be reformed if they find it, such are not to be reputed among Heretics.\+it* And again \+it li. 18 de Civit. c. 51.\+it* ² \+it They that in the Church of Christ have any crazed or perverse opinion, if being admonished to be of a sound and right opinion, they resist obstinately, and will not amend their pestiferous opinions, but persist in defense of them, are thereby become Heretics, and going forth out of the Church, are counted for enemies that exercise us.\+it* Again \+it li. 4. de Bapt. cont. Donat. c. 16. He is an Heretic that, when the doctrine of the Catholic faith is made plain and manifest unto him, had rather resist it, and choose that which himself held &c.\+it* And in diverse places he declareth that S. Cyprian, though he held an error, yet was no Heretic, because he would not have defended it after a general Councel had declared it to be an error. \+it li. 2. de bapt. c. 4.\+it* So Possidonius in the life of S. Augustine ³ reporteth, how, after the determination of the See Apostolic that Pelagius' opinion was heretical, all men esteemed Pelagius an Heretic, and the Emperor made laws against him as against an Heretic. Again S. Augustine saith, \+it He is an Heretic in my opinion, that for some temporal commodity, and specially for his glory and principality, coineth or else followeth false or new opinions.\+it* de utilit. credendi cap. 1. \fq ¹ \ft Who is properly and Heretic, & who is not. \fq ² \ft Descriptions or marks how to know an Heretic. \fq ³ \ft \+it Vit. Aug. c. 18.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 3.10 \ft ¹ Let our Protestants behold themselves in this glass, and withal let them mark all other properties that old Heretics ever had, and they shall find all definitions and marks of an Heretic to fall upon themselves. And therefore they must not marvel if we warn all Catholic men by the words of the Apostle in this place, to take heed of them, and to shun their preachings, books, conventicles and companies. ² Neither need the people be curious to know what they say, much less to confute them: but they must trust God's Church, which doth refute and condemn them. And it is enough for them to know that they be condemned, as S. Augustine noteth in the later end of his book de heresibus. And S. Cyprian ³ saith notably to Antonianus demanding curiously what heresies Novatianus did teach: \+it No matter\+it*, saith he, \+it What heresies he hath or preacheth, when he teacheth without\+it*, that is to say, out of the Church. \fq ¹ \ft The former marks agree to the Protestants. \fq ² \ft Their books, service, & preaching must be avoided. \fq ³ \ft \+it Ep. 52. nu. 7.\+it*\ef*
\v 11 knowing that he that is such an one, is subverted, and sinneth, being condemned by his own judgement. \ef - \fr 3.11 \fq Subverted. \ft ¹ Heretics be often incorrigible, yet the Church of God ceaseth not by all means possible to revoke them. therefore S. Augustine saith \+it ep. 162. The Heretic himself though swelling with odious and detestable pride, and mad with the frowardness of wicked contention, as We admonish that he be avoided lest he deceive the weaklings and little ones, so we refuse not by all means possible to seek his amendment and reformation\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The Church seeketh the amendment of the most obstinate Heretics.\ef* \ef - \fr 3.11 \fq By his own judgement. \ft ¹ Other grievous offenders be separated by excommunication from the communion of Saints and the fellowship of God's Church, by the sentence of their Superiors in the same Church: but Heretics more miserable and infortunate than they, run out of the Church of their own accord, and so give sentence against their own souls to damnation. \fq ¹ \ft Heretics cut themselves from the Church.\ef*
\v 12 When I shall send to thee Artemas or Tychicus, hasten to come unto me to Nicopolis. For there I have determined to winter.
\v 13 Set forward Zenas the lawyer and Apollos carefully, that nothing be wanting to them.
\v 14 And let our men also learn \f 1 \fr 3.14 \ft c προίςασθαι præesse.\f* to excel in good works to necessary uses: that they be not unfruitful.
\v 15 All that are with me, salute thee: salute them that love us in the faith. The grace of God be with you all. Amen.
